and $\\{T^2_n\\}_{n \\geq 17}$ on $n$ vertices, such that $T^1_n$ and $T^2_n$ are non-isomorphic, co-spectral, and the right-angled Coxeter groups (RACGs) based on $T^1_n$ and $T^2_n$ have the same geodesic growth with respect to the standard generating set. We then show that the spectrum of a tree does is not sufficient to determine the geodesic growth of the RACG based on that tree, by providing two infinite families of trees $\\{S^1_n\\}_{n \\geq 11}$ and $\\{S^2_n\\}_{n \\geq 11}$, on $n$ vertices, such that $S^1_n$ and $S^2_n$ are non-isomorphic, co-spectral, and the right-angled Coxeter groups (RACGs) based on $S^1_n$ and $S^2_n$ have distinct geodesic growth. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlexander Kolpakov - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/alexander-kolpakov)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; We prove that for any infinite right-angled Coxeter or Artin group, its spherical and geodesic growth rates (with respect to the standard generating set) either take values in the set of Perron numbers, or equal $1$. ... We then show that the spectrum of a tree does is not sufficient to determine the geodesic growth of the RACG based on that tree, by providing two infinite families of trees $\\{S^1_n\\}_{n \\geq 11}$ and $\\{S^2_n\\}_{n \\geq 11}$, on $n$ vertices, such that $S^1_n$ and $S^2_n$ are non-isomorphic, co-spectral, and the right-angled Coxeter groups (RACGs) based on $S^1_n$ and $S^2_n$ have distinct geodesic growth.\nAlexander Kolpakov, Alexey Talambutsa\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nSpherical and geodesic growth rates of right-angled Coxeter and Artin groups are Perron numbers\n\nPublished • View Publication • BIB\n\nWe prove that for any infinite right-angled Coxeter or Artin group, its spherical and geodesic growth rates (with respect to the standard generating set) either take values in the set of Perron numbers, or equal $1$. Also, we compute the average number of geodesics representing an element of given word length in such groups.\n\ncoxeter group\n\n2017-12-04 v7\n\nRémi Bottinelli, Laura Ciobanu, Alexander Kolpakov\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nThree-dimensional maps and subgroup growth\n\nPublished in Manuscripta Math. (2021) • View Publication • BIB\n\nIn this paper we derive a generating series for the number of cellular complexes known as pavings or three-dimensional maps, on $n$ darts, thus solving an analogue of Tutte's problem in dimension three. The generating series we derive also counts free subgroups of index $n$ in $Δ^+ = \\mathbb{Z}_2*\\mathbb{Z}_2*\\mathbb{Z}_2$ via a simple bijection between pavings and finite index subgroups which can be deduced from the action of $Δ^+$ on the cosets of a given subgroup. We then show that this generating series is non-holonomic. Furthermore, we provide and study the generating series for isomorphism classes of pavings, which correspond to conjugacy classes of free subgroups of finite index in $Δ^+$. Computational experiments performed with software designed by the authors provide some statistics about the topology and combinatorics of pavings on $n\\leq 16$ darts.\n\nsimple bijection\n\n2017-08-13 v4\n\ncoxeter hyperbolic orientable polytope coloring\n\n2015-04-10 v3\n\nLaura Ciobanu, Alexander Kolpakov\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nGeodesic growth of right-angled Coxeter groups based on trees\n\nPublished • View Publication • BIB\n\nIn this paper we exhibit two infinite families of trees $\\{T^1_n\\}_{n \\geq 17}$ and $\\{T^2_n\\}_{n \\geq 17}$ on $n$ vertices, such that $T^1_n$ and $T^2_n$ are non-isomorphic, co-spectral, and the right-angled Coxeter groups (RACGs) based on $T^1_n$ and $T^2_n$ have the same geodesic growth with respect to the standard generating set. We then show that the spectrum of a tree does is not sufficient to determine the geodesic growth of the RACG based on that tree, by providing two infinite families of trees $\\{S^1_n\\}_{n \\geq 11}$ and $\\{S^2_n\\}_{n \\geq 11}$, on $n$ vertices, such that $S^1_n$ and $S^2_n$ are non-isomorphic, co-spectral, and the right-angled Coxeter groups (RACGs) based on $S^1_n$ and $S^2_n$ have distinct geodesic growth. Asymptotically, as $n\\rightarrow \\infty$, each set $T^i_n$, or $S^i_n$, $i=1,2$, has the cardinality of the set of all trees on $n$ vertices. Our proofs are constructive and use two families of trees previously studied by B. McKay and C. Godsil.\n\ncoxeter coxeter group group tree\n\n2014-09-05 v10\n\nAlexander Kolpakov, Leone Slavich\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the area of empty axis-parallel rectangles amidst... (https://arxivlens.com/paperview/details/on-the-area-of-empty-axis-parallel-rectangles-amidst-2-dimensional-lattice-points-2188-1823826)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; 2109.11222 Published Sep 24, 2021\n\nBack ArXiv math.NT On the area of empty axis-parallel rectangles amidst 2-dimensional lattice points\n\nArXiv math.NT\n\n# On the area of empty axis-parallel rectangles amidst 2-dimensional lattice points\n\nJaspar Wiart, Thomas Lachmann\n\n2109.11222 Published Sep 24, 2021\n\nChat with paper Cite 中文 View PDF Share\n\n### Publication\n\nPublished: Sep 24, 2021\n\nCategories: math.NT\n\n### Metrics\n\nSource: ArXiv\n\n### AI Quick Summary\n\nThis research investigates the dispersion of lattices in the plane, defined as the maximum area of empty axis-parallel boxes among lattice points, using continued fraction expansions. The authors provide necessary and sufficient conditions for finite dispersion, derive exact formulas for specific lattices, and offer tight bounds for general lattices based on continued fraction coefficients.\n\n### Paper Preview\n\n### Abstract\n\nThe dispersion of a point set in the unit square is defined to be the area of the largest empty axis-parallel box. In this paper we are interested in the dispersion of lattices in the plane, that is, the supremum of the area of the empty axis-parallel boxes amidst the lattice points. We introduce a framework with which to study this based on the continued fractions expansions of the generators of the lattice. This framework proves so successful that we were unable to ask a question that we could not answer. We give necessary and sufficient conditions under which a lattice has finite dispersion. We obtain an exact formula for the dispersion of the lattices associated to subgroups of the ring of integer of a quadratic field. We have tight bounds for the dispersion of a lattice based the largest continued fraction coefficient of the generators, accurate to within one half. We know what the $n$-th best lattice is. We provide an equivalent formulation of Zaremba's conjecture. Using our framework we are able to give alternative proofs of the results from two other papers in only a few lines.\n\ndispersion dispersion continued lattice lattice generators lattices generators\n\n### Impact\n\n13\n\nReferences\n\n### Paper Details\n\nPaper ID: 2109.11222\n\nLicense: http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\n\nCategories:\n\nmath.NT\n\nView on arXiv Download PDF\n\n### How to Cite This Paper\n\n[Button: BibTeX] [Button: APA] [Button: MLA] [Button: Chicago] [Button: IEEE]\n\nReference manager: RIS EndNote\n\n### Authors\n\nJW Jaspar Wiart 13 papers TL Thomas Lachmann\n\n## Key Terms\n\ndispersion (0.293)\n\ndispersion (0.278)\n\ncontinued (0.241)\n\nlattice (0.236)\n\nlattice (0.233)\n\ngenerators (0.212)\n\nlattices (0.209)\n\ngenerators (0.207)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConjecture on the ordered continued-fraction coefficients of quadratic integers | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/352038/conjecture-on-the-ordered-continued-fraction-coefficients-of)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Thomas Lachmann and Jaspar Wiart, “On the area of empty axis-parallel rectangles amidst 2-dimensional lattice points”, arXiv:2109.11222 (2021).\n\n# Conjecture on the ordered continued-fraction coefficients of quadratic integers\n\n5 years old · traced to 2021\n\nLet\n\n$$\\Delta=[\\overline{a_0,a_1,\\dots,a_{l-1}}]$$Δ=[a 0​,a 1​,…,a l−1​​]\n\nbe a purely periodic quadratic integer, and let $\\mathcal{C}(\\Delta)=\\\\{a_0,a_1,\\dots,a_{l-1}\\\\}$C(Δ)=a 0​,a 1​,…,a l−1​. For each positive integer $n$n, let $w_n$w n​ be the $n$n-th largest element of $\\mathcal{C}(\\Delta)$C(Δ). Quadratic-integer coefficient conjecture. The ordered coefficients satisfy\n\n$$w_n\\leq\\frac{a_0}{n}.$$w n​≤n a 0​​.\n\nThe conjecture proposes quantitative control of all continued fraction coefficients of a purely periodic quadratic integer. The preceding corollary establishes only the bound $a_i\\leq a_0$a i​≤a 0​ for the coefficients of $\\sqrt d$d​; the stronger assertion for general purely periodic quadratic integers is left open in the source.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. Thomas Lachmann and Jaspar Wiart, “On the area of empty axis-parallel rectangles amidst 2-dimensional lattice points”, arXiv:2109.11222 (2021).\n\ncontinued-fractionsquadratic-fields\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Lachmann_T in:math - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3ALachmann_T+in%3Amath)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Sep 24 2021 math.NT arXiv:2109.11222v1\n\n7 results for au:Lachmann_T in:math\n\nShow all abstracts\n\n  * On the area of empty axis-parallel rectangles amidst 2-dimensional lattice points\n\nThomas Lachmann, Jaspar Wiart\n\nSep 24 2021 math.NT arXi